Source: https://salsa.debian.org/samueloph/distributary and https://github.com/samueloph/distributary

Showcase, with Forest (the default, shown here) and seven more colours (each dark and light, and all but Lineage also without the accent on the fork), a live palette editor and downloads: https://samueloph.dev/debian-distributary-theme/

== Generated by a program ==

Apart from the Debian logo and Lato's letter shapes, nothing in this theme is drawn by hand or made by an image-generating model. Every wallpaper, login screen, boot splash frame, GRUB background, installer banner, HDR master and video is computed by a Python program that uses only packages from Debian. The program is the preferred form for modification: change a palette or the river's seed in `src/distributary/design.py`, run `make`, and the whole theme follows. Builds are reproducible (byte-identical), and `make check` compares the SVGs in librsvg, Qt SVG, resvg and Inkscape.

== Concept ==

In hydrology, a ''distributary'' is a channel that forks away from a river and flows on toward the sea. It is the opposite of a tributary. Debian is the river much of the Linux world branches from: hundreds of distributions are built on it, and more on those, forks of forks. So for ''forky'' this theme celebrates the fork itself: a single stream splits, then splits again, into a luminous delta at dusk.

 * Every junction is a Y, so there are forks everywhere: no cutlery, and no Forky, the Toy Story character.
 * The light sits on the first fork (the accent on the fork): the water is nearly white where the river splits, then turns gold and sage green as the delta spreads. The Y of ''forky'' is the brightest point in the picture.
 * Faint contour lines turn the land between the channels into islands, like a map.
 * The fork sits on the left third line, where the eye lands first. The corners on the left stay calm for icons and windows, and the fine detail fades out to the right.
 * The '''dark''' version is the default. The '''light''' version is its counterpart, for GNOME's light/dark wallpaper switching and for KDE colour schemes. There the river is ink on pale paper, and the accent on the fork is its deepest ink instead of a light.

== Process ==

The river is grown by a small program following the rules of real deltas:

 * channels meander;
 * they steer away from each other;
 * they split with flow conserved: the widths follow a generalised [[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murray%27s_law|Murray's law]] (exponent 2.4);
 * a channel that runs into another thins out and ends, like a silted-up side arm.

The main stem and the first three forks are art-directed; everything downstream grows from a fixed seed (6).

Everything comes from one description of the scene: the desktop-base SVGs, high-resolution PNGs up to 8K, and the HDR masters. The high-resolution PNGs are the shipped SVGs rendered by librsvg over a floating-point base, so they cannot drift apart. The SVGs use only gradients, group opacity and one embedded image (no filters), so Qt SVG (KDE) can render them too.

== Related work ==

Rivers and their branching have been drawn before. The closest work we know of, for comparison:

 * Daniel Coe, ''4K Rivers'': real rivers and deltas rendered from high-resolution elevation data, some in glowing channels on dark ground. https://dancoecarto.com/4k-rivers
 * Robert Hodgin, ''Meander'' (2020): a program that grows fictional meandering rivers and draws them as maps. https://roberthodgin.com/project/meander

No picture was traced or used as input: Distributary's river is grown by its own program (see [[#Process|Process]]).

